>
<p>An SEO audit consultant investigates a defined organic-search problem and explains what the evidence supports. The engagement should name the consultant or review team, scope, access, deliverables and decision that the report must inform.</p>
<p>Choose consultation when the problem needs discussion and iteration. Choose a fixed audit service when the question and output are already clear.</p>

<h2 id="when-to-hire-one">When does an SEO audit consultant help?</h2>
<p>A consultant helps when the site has an unexplained search decline, a planned migration, complex rendering or conflicting recommendations. The consultant should form hypotheses, collect relevant evidence and state which causes remain unproven.</p>
<p>Google's <a href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/crawling-indexing/site-move-with-url-changes">site-move guidance</a> calls for URL mapping, redirects, canonical checks and updated sitemaps. A migration consultation can turn those requirements into a site-specific review plan.</p>

<h2 id="what-an-seo-audit-consultant-does">What should the consultant deliver?</h2>
<p>The consultant should deliver a scope record, evidence log, prioritised findings and verification plan. Each finding should name the affected URLs and separate observation from likely impact.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Diagnosis:</strong> test agreed causes against crawl, indexation and performance evidence.</li>
  <li><strong>Priorities:</strong> rank actions by evidence, dependency, effort and business importance.</li>
  <li><strong>Handoff:</strong> describe the change and the observable check that closes the finding.</li>
  <li><strong>Limits:</strong> record missing access, incomplete samples and questions outside scope.</li>
</ul>
<p>Review the <a href="/website-audit-services/report-example/">website audit report example</a> to see a dated evidence format.</p>

<h2 id="consultant-vs-agency-vs-software">Should you choose a consultant, fixed service or software?</h2>
<p>Choose by the decision you need to make. Software collects repeatable data.</p>
<p>A fixed service interprets an agreed scope. A consultant supports an investigation that may change as evidence appears.</p>
<div class="overflow-x-auto" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Comparison of SEO audit options">
<table><thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Best use</th><th>Buyer must confirm</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Consultant</td><td>Complex diagnosis or migration planning</td><td>Named contact, time, outputs and follow-up</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fixed audit service</td><td>Defined one-off review</td><td>Page sample, checks, report and delivery date</td></tr>
<tr><td>Software</td><td>Repeatable in-house data collection</td><td>Who validates and prioritises the output</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>

<h2 id="pricing">How much does AuditWeb's audit support cost?</h2>
<p>AuditWeb's basic audit starts at $297 USD and its comprehensive audit starts at $997 USD. Consultation, migration support, large sites and ongoing work use a custom quote based on time and deliverables.</p>
<p>Confirm whether the quote covers investigation only, a written report, meetings, implementation advice and later verification. Rankings and recovery dates cannot be guaranteed.</p>

<h2 id="faq">What should you ask before hiring?</h2>
<h3>Will one named person do the work?</h3><p>Ask the provider to identify who investigates, reviews and presents the findings. AuditWeb confirms the delivery arrangement during scoping.</p>
<h3>Does Search Console access matter?</h3><p>Read-only Search Console access can add indexing and performance evidence. Public-page checks remain possible without it. The scope should state the limitation.</p>
<h3>Does consultation include implementation?</h3><p>Implementation is separate unless the written quote includes it.</p>
